{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 50, :comments ["<b>And to Joseph were born, etc.</b> The verse connects with what precedes it, to tell of the abundant good that came to Joseph in the seven years of plenty: for besides the fact that he amassed much grain and succeeded, he was also remembered for good with children, and the naming of his sons thus declares that he was sated with favor, for HaShem relieved him of all his toil and HaShem made him fruitful — and this fruitfulness includes children, wealth, and honor." "<b>Before the year of, etc.</b> In tractate Ta'anit (Ta'anit 11a) they, of blessed memory, expounded from here that it is forbidden to be intimate with one's wife in years of famine. And one must say that their exposition is according to the principle of 'if it does not apply to the matter at hand' (i.e., it is not literally about Joseph), for he had not yet fulfilled the commandment of procreation, and they said there in the Talmud as an explanation that those who are childless are permitted to be intimate with their wives in years of famine, and we do not find that Joseph had children before Ephraim and Manasseh, and according to the law, even during the seven years of famine he should have been intimate — but the verse is expounded according to the principle of 'if it does not apply.'" "And from this it is no longer difficult what Tosafot asked there regarding Jochebed, who was born between the walls — that Levi was intimate during the years of famine — for when our forefathers went down to Egypt it was the second year of the famine, and they were forced to say that the Sages taught a measure of piety and Levi was not a pietist in this matter. And their words do not appear correct — not in what they said about it being a measure of piety, for the Sages derived it from Scripture, and not in what emerged from their words, that Levi was not a pietist. The Torah testified about him (Deuteronomy 33:8): ״לְאִישׁ חֲסִידֶךָ״ — for he was the pietist among the tribes, and although he said this about his seed, the branches testify about the root. And the difficulty of Tosafot is no difficulty, for the exposition of the Sages is according to the principle of 'if it does not apply,' and Levi, since he lacked a female child [i.e., had not yet fulfilled procreation], did not refrain from being intimate with his wife according to the law (Yevamot 61a; Even HaEzer, sec. 1)." "<b>Further,</b> they did not state the prohibition of this thing except on account of the distress of Israel, and not on account of the distress of the nations when they are starving, for they are of no concern either way. And then Levi had no obligation to refrain from himself on account of the calamity of the nations, for Jacob had grain, as they, of blessed memory, said (Ta'anit 10b), and he did not send his sons to buy grain except for the sake of appearances, as it is written (Genesis 42:1): ״לָמָּה תִּתְרָאוּ״. And although they said (Genesis Rabbah 91) that they paid full prices in the market, this was after the first year. But in the first year they surely had grain, and therefore it was permitted for him to be intimate. And as for Joseph, there was no reason for them to refrain on account of concern over an individual's famine. And Joseph, had there not been the reason that he was childless, it is possible that he would have refrained on account of concern for his father and brothers, that they might starve, and they were many."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 41, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:47:58.414441077Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}